            <ArticleTitle>[A case of perforated small bowel adenocarcinoma as first symptom of Crohn's disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>212-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A case of perforated small bowel adenocarcinoma presenting as first symptom of Crohn's disease is reported in a 53 years old male patient with abdominal pain and alteration of bowel habits. Endoscopic bioptical sampling demonstrated a Crohn's disease in active stage. The patient underwent medical therapy with resolution of the complained symptomatology; 20 days after discharge the patients is rehospitalized for acute abdomen. A narrowing perforated neoplasia in terminal ileum was discovered at laparotomy. Diffuse peritonitis and metastatic peritoneal implantations were also revealed. An ileocolic resection is performed with ileo-transverse colon anastomosis. The patient dies 4 months later for advanced disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
